; expect 157
; A free identifier in a macro's template means what it meant where the
; macro was WRITTEN, not where it was called.
;
; It used to mean whichever one the call site could see, so one macro
; had two meanings. `MacScope.callHelper` expands to `(helper x)`;
; invoked from this file, which defines its own `helper`, it reached
; THIS file's, and invoked from inside `MacScope.viaOwn` it reached
; `MacScope`'s. Same macro, same template, two answers, chosen by the
; caller's module. Neither compiler diagnosed it, because both were
; resolving a name that really was in scope.
;
; Four terms, each failing differently if resolution is wrong:
;
;   (callHelper 5)               50 - the macro crossing a module
;                                     boundary. The old rule gave 6.
;   (viaOwn 5)                   50 - the same macro inside its own
;                                     module, which was already right
;                                     and must stay right.
;   (helper 6)                    7 - this file's own reference to its
;                                     own definition, which must not
;                                     have moved.
;   (let ((helper 0)) ...)       50 - the reverse half of hygiene: a
;                                     LOCAL binding of the template's
;                                     free name must not capture it
;                                     either. Under the old rule this
;                                     was AX3004 `expected function
;                                     type, found Int`, so the term is
;                                     also what proves the fix is
;                                     resolution and not luck.
;
; 50 + 50 + 7 + 50 = 157. Kept under 256 deliberately: a case's whole
; answer is its exit status, and the status is one byte - an earlier
; draft of this file totalled 322 and reported 66.
;
; The boundary this did NOT cross, closed on 2026-08-16 and kept here
; because the reasoning is what explains the shape: THIS file's
; protection is a NAME. `resolveImports` mangles an imported
; declaration's name to `Mod$name`, and that mangled name is what a
; module template's free identifier resolves to; an entry-file
; declaration is left bare, so there was no distinct name to resolve
; to and a local captured it - silently, then as `AX3032`, now
; retired. It resolves by a second route instead of by a name: a macro
; is a top-level declaration, so `substTpl` stamps every template free
; identifier and both resolvers skip the local scope for a stamped
; reference. `tests/selfhost/394-macro-entry-capture.ax` (130)
; measures it. See docs/macro-system.md section 3.
(import MacScope)

(:: helper (-> Int Int))

(fn (helper n) (+ n 1))

(:: main Int)

(fn (main)
  (+
    (callHelper 5)    (+
      (viaOwn 5)      (+
        (helper 6)        (let ((helper 0))
          (callHelper 5)
        )
      )
    )
  )
)
